  "number": "5.1",
  "title": "",
  "body": "  The Koszul complex is exact in positive degrees. Every differential is continuous and strict onto its image, and every image is closed. In particular, if then is closed. Moreover, there exists such that There also exists such that    Lean (koszulGraph_restricted_exact)  Lean (koszulDifferential_continuous)  Lean (koszulDifferential_isStrict)  Lean (isClosed_range_koszulDifferential)  Lean (pow_smul_graphIdeal_inter_unitBall_subset)  Lean (pow_smul_ker_d1_inter_subset)   Over the polynomial ring , the sequence has Koszul complex exact in positive degrees. Indeed, this may be checked after localising at a prime. If one of the is not in the prime, it is a unit after localisation and the Koszul complex is contractible. If all of them lie in the prime, then does not: otherwise all the would also lie in the prime, contrary to . Thus is invertible there and The elements form a regular sequence, since they are monic linear polynomials in distinct variables. This proves the assertion over the polynomial ring.  The map is flat. In fact, is noetherian for , so is noetherian and its -adic completion is flat over it . Inverting gives the displayed flat map. Tensoring the polynomial Koszul complex with , and using the standard base-change identification of its finite free terms, proves positive-degree exactness. Notice that this does not assert exactness in degree zero.  Each differential is continuous because its coordinates are finite sums of multiplication maps. The image of is closed by the noetherian closed-ideal theorem. In higher degree, exactness identifies an image with the kernel of the next continuous differential, and hence that image is closed. The nonarchimedean open mapping theorem therefore shows that, for each differential , there is a constant such that every has a preimage satisfying This also proves that each differential is strict onto its image.  Choose such that . If , choose with Then and , which proves . Similarly, choose such that . If , choose with and . Then , proving .   "
