  "body": " Finite-variable subrings   For every , substitution gives a norm-preserving ring isomorphism For this is the evident identification of both sides with . Every can be written Consequently is finite over and is a strongly noetherian Tate ring. The inclusions for preserve the norm. Moreover, for every and there are and such that In particular,    Lean (even Tate algebra)  Lean (norm preservation)  Lean (finite decomposition)  Lean (finite module)  Lean (Tate structure)  Lean (strong noetherianity)  Lean (nested finite-variable rings)  Lean (isometric inclusion)  Lean (approximation and density)   On even exponents, halving the -coordinates identifies the support monoid with that of the Tate algebra ; the coefficient map and its inverse preserve the Gauss norm. Write each exponent uniquely as , with . Since , every allowed monomial factors as Grouping the monomials with the same tuple gives . The finitely many therefore generate over . The same argument after adjoining any finite number of Tate variables gives strong noetherianity. The inclusions are coefficientwise and hence isometric. Finally, approximate to the required precision by a finite sum of monomials and choose so that only occur in that sum. This gives the stated estimate and .   "
