  "number": "8.6",
  "title": "Coefficientwise localisation.",
  "body": " Coefficientwise localisation   Let be a rational datum whose entries lie in , and put . There is a bicontinuous ring isomorphism The ring on the right has the supremum norm, and its multiplication is given by , with replaced by its image in . If a rational refinement is also represented by data in and is the corresponding restriction map, then the induced restriction sends    Lean (coefficientwise equivalence)  Lean (continuity)  Lean (continuous inverse)  Lean (restriction is coefficientwise)   Apply the canonical map to every coefficient in . This gives a continuous homomorphism In the target, the image of is a unit and each is of norm at most , by the defining property of . Thus extends continuously to for the rational-localisation topology, and then to the completion:   In the other direction, the inclusion gives a continuous homomorphism . Each lies in , and the canonical map to a rational localisation is bounded. Hence the family is bounded in . Apply the homomorphism to every coefficient. If , the series converges in . The resulting map is continuous, and shows that it is multiplicative.  The composite fixes every term , and hence every finite sum of such terms. These finite sums are dense. The other composite fixes the dense image of . Continuity therefore gives and . This proves the claimed isomorphism.  If comes from a rational refinement, all the maps just used commute with this restriction map. On a finite sum, restriction simply applies to each coefficient; continuity gives the stated formula for every convergent sum.   "
